+# Persistent directory access (Host API 1.5)
+
+This is a generic host filesystem capability for UI-only plugins. The host does
+not know database documents, snapshot formats, indexes, compression, or sync times.
+Desktop integration: Tauri 2 / Rust, Vue 3 / TypeScript. The current macOS build
+has no App Sandbox entitlement; Windows is a conventional Win32 desktop build.
+Browser hosts advertise `capabilities.directoryAccess === false`. Sandboxed OS
+packages that require bookmarks or portal tokens need a platform credential
+adapter before they can advertise persistent support; a path is not such a token.
+
+## Consent and ownership
+
+Declare `host.directory:read` and, for writes, `host.directory:write`. Check
+`capabilities.directoryAccess`. A native picker names the installed plugin,
+requested mode, and persistence. An `initialPath` only positions that picker;
+no API opens an arbitrary root supplied by a plugin. Cancel returns `null`.
+
+The host stores grants in its own `directory-grants/grants.json`, outside plugin
+UI storage. Entries bind random UUIDs to plugin ID, publisher, installer signing
+key ID, repository provenance, mode, and directory identity. On Unix the identity
+is device + inode; on Windows it is volume + file index. The file is atomically
+replaced with private permissions. `setPreferred(id)` can persist one default
+directory per principal, independently of UI-storage quotas. New grants do not
+change that preference; a plugin selects its default after completing any copy
+or validation work. Revocation preserves the default tombstone to prevent
+silent fallback to an older directory. Plugin callers receive display metadata and an
+opaque ID, not the host record. A new grant is independent of older handles.
+
+The trusted iframe bridge supplies the caller ID; native commands recheck the
+installed manifest and derive ownership from installer provenance. A plugin ID
+inside request parameters never selects the caller. The selected directory may
+not contain the host authorization store. Neither the manifest permission nor a
+saved path grants filesystem access by itself.
+
+On restart, `restore(id)` opens the original directory without prompting and
+compares its identity. Missing/unmounted directories return `unavailable` and
+are never recreated. A replacement at the same path returns `identityChanged`.
+Revoked grants return `revoked`; unknown or changed plugin principals receive
+`[NEEDS_AUTHORIZATION]`. Every operation revalidates the grant and directory, and
+OS access checks still apply. Effective file permissions can change between
+operations, so successful restore does not guarantee a subsequent write.
+
+Plugin Center → installed plugin → Directory access lists active grants,
+including grants held by prior signing identities, and can revoke them. Revocation
+is serialized with commits, persists before returning, and closes staged handles.
+It does not delete user files. `listGrants()` includes revoked tombstones so a UI
+recovering its preferred directory never silently falls back to an older grant.
+
+## UI SDK
+
+```js
+const api = window.dbxPlugin;
+await api.ready;
+if (!api.capabilities.directoryAccess) throw new Error('unsupported host');
+
+// Only from the plugin's explicit user action:
+const grant = await api.directory.request({
+  mode: 'readwrite', persistence: 'required', initialPath: '/optional/picker/hint'
+});
+if (!grant) return; // cancellation keeps previous settings
+// After application-specific validation/copy succeeds:
+await api.directory.setPreferred(grant.id);
+
+// Store just grant.id in UI preferences. On subsequent launches:
+const restored = await api.directory.restore(grant.id);
+if (restored.status !== 'ready') return; // display recovery action, no prompt loop
+const current = await api.directory.stat(grant.id, 'example.json');
+const upload = await api.directory.beginWrite(grant.id, 'example.json', {
+  expectedEtag: current.etag // null means create only; never blind overwrite
+});
+try {
+  // Split bytes into <= upload.chunkBytes and base64-encode each chunk.
+  await api.directory.write(upload.handleId, 0, dataBase64);
+  await api.directory.commit(upload.handleId, sha256OfCompleteBytes);
+} catch (error) {
+  await api.directory.abort(upload.handleId).catch(() => {});
+  throw error;
+}
+```
+
+All methods return promises. `Grant` includes `id`, `displayName`, `displayPath`,
+`mode`, `persistent`, `preferred`, `createdAt` (Unix seconds), `status`, `chunkBytes`,
+`maxFileBytes`. Display paths are informational.
+
+| Method | Result / behavior |
+| --- | --- |
+| `request({mode,persistence?,initialPath?})` | `Grant \| null`; native consent |
+| `listGrants()` | This principal's grants in creation order, including revoked records |
+| `setPreferred(grantId)` | Persist this principal's default directory; no file data changes |
+| `restore(grantId)` | Grant with `ready`, `unavailable`, `identityChanged`, `revoked` |
+| `revoke(grantId)` | Revoke, close in-flight streams; leave files |
+| `list(grantId,path='',cursor?)` | `{entries:[{name,kind}],cursor}`; 500 entries/page; skip symlinks and own temporary files |
+| `stat(grantId,path)` | `{etag:string\|null}`; SHA-256, null if missing |
+| `mkdir(grantId,path)` | Create one relative directory |
+| `openRead(grantId,path)` | `{handleId,size,etag,chunkBytes}` |
+| `read(handleId,offset,length)` | `{dataBase64,length,eof}` |
+| `close(handleId)` | Close read or discard staged write |
+| `beginWrite(grantId,path,{expectedEtag})` | `{handleId,chunkBytes}` |
+| `write(handleId,offset,dataBase64)` | `{written}`; sequential chunks |
+| `commit(handleId,sha256)` | `{committed:true,etag}` |
+| `abort(handleId)` | Discard staged bytes |
+
+Paths must be relative, with `/` components. Absolute, traversal, backslash,
+colon, control characters, ambiguous trailing dots/spaces and DOS device names
+are rejected. Filesystem resolution uses `cap-std` directory capabilities,
+including handle-relative parent resolution and rename, rather than string
+prefix checks. Reads accept regular files; Unix opens are nonblocking to avoid
+FIFO hangs. Handles are principal-bound, limited (16/principal, 128 total) and
+expire after ten idle minutes. Workbench reload/close releases its own handles.
+
+Each chunk is <= 512 KiB. Each file is <= 512 MiB. Staged files are created
+privately in the target parent. Commit verifies the full SHA-256, rechecks the
+original parent identity and target etag, flushes the file and atomically renames
+it over the destination. Hash, conflict, revocation, write and flush failures
+before rename discard staging and retain the previous file. A crash can leave a
+hidden `.dbx-write-*` file; it is never a committed document. The host serializes
+its own writers. Etag checks do not provide cross-process CAS against arbitrary
+external programs modifying the same file; prefer a dedicated plugin directory.
+Use local filesystems with atomic replacement semantics for durable snapshots.
+
+## Failure handling
+
+Errors preserve `[CODE]` prefixes across the bridge. Applications should retain
+previous committed files and success timestamps, and must not retry as blind
+writes or auto-recreate missing roots.
+
+- `REVOKED`, `NEEDS_AUTHORIZATION`, `IDENTITY_CHANGED`: explicit reauthorization.
+- `UNAVAILABLE`: keep grant and retry after the volume/directory returns.
+- `READ_ONLY`, `ACCESS_DENIED`: retain readable data; correct mode or OS rights.
+- `CONFLICT`: reload before deciding how to merge or replace.
+- `DISK_FULL`, `IO_ERROR`, `CHECKSUM_MISMATCH`: abort the upload; preserve old data.
+- `NOT_FOUND`: handle a missing file independently of database/network access.
+- `TOO_LARGE`, `BUSY`, `INVALID_ARGUMENT`, `INVALID_PATH`, `INVALID_HANDLE`: fix the
+  request, bound concurrency or reopen an expired stream.
+
+Atomicity is per file. Multi-file transactions and application data validation
+belong to the plugin. An app wanting complete snapshots must stage the entire
+snapshot in memory before writing, and update its successful-sync state only
+when commit succeeds. Unknown snapshot format versions must be preserved.
